    "indications_en": "Ondansetron is a serotonin subtype 3 (5-HT3) receptor antagonist indicated: • Prevention of nausea and vomiting associated with initial and repeat courses of emetogenic cancer chemotherapy. • Prevention and treatment of post-operative nausea and vomiting. • Prevention of radiotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.",
    "dosage_en": "Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea and Vomiting- Adults, Pediatric patients (6 months to 18 years): • 8 mg tablet/orodispersible tablet: Three 0.15 mg/kg doses, up to a maximum of 16 mg per dose. • 4 mg orodispersible tablet: Three 0.15 mg/kg doses, up to a maximum of 16 mg per dose. • Injection: Three 0.15 mg/kg doses, up to a maximum of 16 mg per dose, infused intravenously over 15 minutes. Radiotherap...",
    "side_effects_en": "Frequently reported adverse events were headache, constipation and diarrhea, but the majority have been mild or moderate in nature. In chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, rash has occurred in approximately 1% of patients receiving Ondansetron. There also have been reports to a sensation of flushing or warmth, hiccups and liver enzyme abnormal...",
    "precautions_en": "Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported in patients who have exhibited hypersensitivity to other selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonists. Ondansetron is not a drug that stimulates gastric or intestinal peristalsis. It should not be used instead of nasogastric suction. The use of Ondansetron in patients following abdominal surgery or in patients...",
    "how_it_works_en": "Ondansetron is a potent, highly selective 5HT3 receptor-antagonist. Its precise mode of action in the control of nausea and vomiting is not known. Chemotherapeutic agents and radiotherapy may cause release of 5HT in the small intestine initiating a vomiting reflex by activating vagal afferents via 5HT3 receptors. Ondansetron blocks the initiation of this reflex. Activation of vagal afferents may a...",
    "pregnancy_en": "Carcinogenic effects were not seen in 2-year studies in rats and mice with oral Ondansetron doses up to 10 and 30 mg/kg per day, respectively. Ondansetron was not mutagenic in standard tests for mutagenicity. Oral administration of Ondansetron up to...",
